WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.480
Open a social app and pull down to refresh.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.457
In under a second, you see the newest posts from the 200 accounts you follow.

3
00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:14.558
Then a pop singer with 31 million followers posts a photo.

4
00:00:14.558 --> 00:00:18.449
For the next few minutes, posts arrive late for everybody.

5
00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.145
Even the café's post about today's cake, which only 300 people follow.

6
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:26.689
Why would one person's post make everyone else's late?

7
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:30.380
Picture the app as the mail room of a big office.

8
00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.316
The simplest plan keeps one pile of letters for each sender.

9
00:00:34.316 --> 00:00:36.808
One for Ana, one for Ben, one for Eli.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.147
When Cleo comes for her mail, a clerk goes through the pile of everyone she follows, and picks out what's new.

11
00:00:44.147 --> 00:00:45.252
Three piles here.

12
00:00:45.252 --> 00:00:46.876
On a real app, about 200.

13
00:00:47.033 --> 00:00:51.559
That's called fan-out on read: the work happens when someone reads.

14
00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:53.045
And people read a lot.

15
00:00:53.045 --> 00:01:00.408
With 2 million refreshes a second, each looking through about 200 piles, that's 400 million lookups a second.

16
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.767
Wouldn't it be nice if Cleo's mail were already sorted when she came for it?

17
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:06.427
So turn it around.

18
00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:10.295
Give everyone a pigeonhole, their own small box on the wall.

19
00:01:10.295 --> 00:01:16.807
The moment a letter arrives, a clerk sorts it into the pigeonhole of everyone who follows its sender.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:17.776
Ana posts.

21
00:01:17.776 --> 00:01:23.755
Her followers are Cleo, Ben and Eli, so her post goes into three pigeonholes.

22
00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:24.764
Three writes.

23
00:01:24.933 --> 00:01:26.730
Now Cleo comes for her mail.

24
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.451
One lookup, of a list that's already sorted, newest first.

25
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:36.888
In the app, each pigeonhole is a ready-made timeline: a list of post numbers, kept for every user.

26
00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:42.221
This design is called fan-out on write, because the work happens when someone posts.

27
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.217
It's a copy, worked out from the posts and the follows, so it could be thrown away and rebuilt.

28
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:53.912
It exists only to make reads cheap, and you pay for it on every post: one write per follower.

29
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.774
Let's count it with the same round numbers.

30
00:01:57.774 --> 00:02:04.865
5,800 posts a second, each to 200 followers, is about 1.16 million writes a second.

31
00:02:04.865 --> 00:02:10.163
Against 400 million lookups, that's about 345 times less work.

32
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:11.355
Why such a gap?

33
00:02:11.355 --> 00:02:12.606
Look at one author.

34
00:02:12.606 --> 00:02:14.977
The follower count is on both sides.

35
00:02:14.977 --> 00:02:19.258
So the question is which happens more often: posting, or reading.

36
00:02:19.258 --> 00:02:23.605
People refresh their feeds far more often than their friends post.

37
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:25.157
Bursts are fine too.

38
00:02:25.157 --> 00:02:34.957
If 150,000 posts land in one second, the letters wait in a queue, and the clerks, called fan-out workers, catch up over the next few minutes.

39
00:02:34.957 --> 00:02:38.293
Posts appear a little late, and reads stay fast.

40
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:41.189
Twitter ran this design in 2012.

41
00:02:41.189 --> 00:02:47.909
It served about 300,000 timeline reads a second, against about 5,000 new posts.

42
00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:52.801
The goal: every post in every follower's timeline within 5 seconds.

43
00:02:52.801 --> 00:02:56.424
Reaching a million followers took about 3.5 seconds.

44
00:02:56.633 --> 00:02:58.213
Now the singer posts.

45
00:02:58.213 --> 00:03:00.395
She had 31 million followers.

46
00:03:00.395 --> 00:03:02.878
So one post is 31 million writes.

47
00:03:02.878 --> 00:03:06.940
At a million every 3.5 seconds, how long does it take?

48
00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:08.144
Pause and guess.

49
00:03:11.333 --> 00:03:12.733
About 108 seconds.

50
00:03:12.733 --> 00:03:16.232
In practice, deliveries took up to 5 minutes.

51
00:03:16.433 --> 00:03:19.975
And the clerks take letters from the queue in order.

52
00:03:19.975 --> 00:03:25.151
So the café's post, with only 300 followers, waits behind 31 million writes.

53
00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:31.988
That's the celebrity problem: one account with so many followers that its work holds up everyone else's.

54
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:39.183
In the mail room, one sender writes to 31 million people at once, and the clerks do nothing else all morning.

55
00:03:39.633 --> 00:03:42.068
So the average told the wrong story.

56
00:03:42.068 --> 00:03:46.396
The average account has about 200 followers, and looks harmless.

57
00:03:46.396 --> 00:03:49.168
The design is decided by the largest one.

58
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.669
The cost of a post is its author's follower count.

59
00:03:52.669 --> 00:03:54.043
Find the largest one.

60
00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:58.834
The fix comes next in the lesson: don't sort the giant's letters at all.

61
00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:03.735
Leave them on a table by the door, and let each follower pick one up on the way out.

62
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.108
One quick check.

63
00:04:05.108 --> 00:04:07.674
The café posts about today's cake.

64
00:04:07.674 --> 00:04:09.259
It has 300 followers.

65
00:04:09.259 --> 00:04:11.750
How many timeline writes is that?

66
00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:12.957
Pause and think.

67
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.877
300, one per follower.

68
00:04:17.877 --> 00:04:18.868
That's cheap.

69
00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:23.900
It was late only because it waited behind the singer's 31 million.
